<InstitutionalPage
  title="Editorial Standards"
  eyebrow="Standards"
  description="Nuvellum distinguishes reporting, analysis, opinion, reviews and sponsored work, with greater scrutiny where the stakes are higher."
  intro="Standards ought to be visible in the work itself: in what is named, what is sourced, what is attributed and what is withheld until it can be established."
  note="These principles apply across every desk and every published form."
>
  <h2>Labels are part of the work</h2>
  <p>Reporting, analysis, opinion, reviews and sponsored material are presented as distinct forms. Reporting should not borrow the certainty of commentary, and commercial material should never pass itself off as independent editorial judgment.</p>
  <h2>Sources and attribution</h2>
  <p>The origin of a material claim should be capable of being traced. Allegations, disputed interpretations and contested accounts belong to the people or institutions making them; they should be attributed as such, not absorbed into Nuvellum’s own voice.</p>
  <h2>Sensitive stories</h2>
  <p>Political, legal, conflict, serious-harm and other high-risk subjects demand a higher threshold of verification. Where that threshold is not met, publication waits.</p>
  <h2>Editorial responsibility</h2>
  <p>Every published piece is answerable to the same obligations of sourcing, attribution, verification and correction. Format may change; responsibility does not.</p>
</InstitutionalPage>
